# Cache rules for the Worker's static assets.
#
# The default Workers Assets header is `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`,
# which only works if the response carries a validator. Pages do not: a page
# comes back with an ETag on the asset itself but none on the document the
# Worker returns, so Cloudflare caches the document at the edge, has nothing
# to revalidate it against, and keeps serving it after a deploy. Staff kept
# seeing old copy and old photographs until they hard-refreshed -- and a hard
# refresh was only fixing their own browser, not the edge copy everyone else
# was getting.
#
# So: documents are never stored, and everything with a content-hashed name is
# stored for good. Rules are listed least-specific first.

/*
  Cache-Control: no-store

# Astro writes the hash into the filename, so a changed file is a new URL and
# the old one is never requested again.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Photographs and brand art ship under their own names and can change without
# the name changing, so they keep the revalidate-every-time default -- which
# works here, because these are served straight from assets and do carry an
# ETag. A 304 is a few hundred bytes; the file is a few hundred kilobytes.
/hero/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/brand/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
